#{[17592186594151 "$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}y+\\frac{1}{3}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ for the continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is shown above. Given that \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{11}{18},\\,\\text{SD}[X]≈0.2582,\\,\\text{SD}[Y]≈0.2664,\n$$\n\n \nfind the correlation coefficient of $X$ and $Y.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places."] [17592186594155 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-2x-y}, & \\,x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ for $x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0.$"] [17592186594164 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following conditional probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\,0<x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{2}\\,x \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(\\frac{1}{4}<Y\\,|\\,X=\\frac{3}{2}).$"] [17592186594182 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3x}{8}, & \\,0\\le x<2,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le x+2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ for $0\\le x<2,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le x+2.$"] [17592186594191 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{xy+1}{3}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ for $0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1.$"] [17592186594193 "$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2y}{1-{x}^{2}}, & 0\\le x<1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{15{x}^{2}(1-{x}^{2})}{2}, & 0\\le x<1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the continuous random variables $X$ and $Y,$ for which the conditional probability density function ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ and the marginal density function ${f}_{X}(x)$ are shown above. Find the expression of the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ for $0\\le x<1,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}.$"] [17592186594202 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following conditional probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+2y}{2y+1}, & \\,0<x<1,\\,0<y<1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X<\\frac{3}{4}\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{1}{2}).$"] [17592186594218 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3(x+2y)}{2}, & \\,0\\le y\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFind $P(X<\\frac{2}{5}\\,|\\,Y=\\frac{1}{5}).$"] [17592186594224 "$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x+{e}^{y}}{e}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ for the continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is shown above. Given that \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{3e+1}{6e},\\,\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{3}{2e},\\,\\text{SD}[X]≈0.2821,\\,\\text{SD}[Y]≈0.2870,\n$$\n\n \nfind the correlation coefficient of $X$ and $Y.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places."] [17592186594229 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x{e}^{y}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 1,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡2 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFind $P(X<Y\\,|\\,X=\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)).$"] [17592186594232 "$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{xy+2}{4}, & \\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ for the continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is shown above. Given that \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{1}{12},\\,\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\text{SD}[X]≈0.5713,\\,\\text{SD}[Y]≈0.2887,\n$$\n\n \nfind the correlation coefficient of $X$ and $Y.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places."] [17592186594234 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following joint probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{21{x}^{2}y}{2}, & \\,-1<x\\le 0,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ for $-1<x\\le 0,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 1.$"] [17592186594238 "$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9({x}^{2}{y}^{2}+1)}{16}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3({y}^{2}+3)}{16}, & 1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the continuous random variables $X$ and $Y,$ for which the joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ and the marginal density function ${f}_{Y}(y)$ are shown above. Find the expression of the conditional probability density function ${f}_{X\\,|\\,Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ for $0\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le y\\le 2.$"] [17592186594674 "Find $\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)$ and $\\lim_{x\\to {(\\pi /2)}^{+}}f(x)$ for the function given by\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & \\text{ for }\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<x<0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x+x & \\text{ for }\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le x<\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186594701 "Find the right-sided limit at $x=2$ for the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-16\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x\\le 2 \\\\ \\sqrt{2x+5}-\\sqrt{2x}\\, & \\text{ for }\\,x>2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}f(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186594714 "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curve ${x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1.$ If $R=[-1,1]\\times [-4,4],$ and\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}|{x}^{3}y-{y}^{2}|\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of $f(x,y)?$\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{aligned}0, & (x,y)\\in D∖R \\\\ |{x}^{3}y-{y}^{2}|, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{aligned}|{x}^{3}y-{y}^{2}|, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\notin D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle \\{\\begin{aligned}|{x}^{3}y-{y}^{2}|, & (x,y)\\in D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186594725 "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curves $y={x}^{2}$ and $y=3-|x|.$ If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [0,3]$\n\nII. $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{R}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-2,2]\\times [1,3]$\n\nIII. $$\n\\displaystyle {∬}_{D}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\\,\n$$ where $R=[-1,1]\\times [0,3]$"] [17592186595919 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{9}x, & \\,0\\le x\\le 3, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y, & \\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X\\le 2,Y\\le 3).$"] [17592186595922 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2y}{(x+1)^{2}}, & x\\ge 0,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expected value of $Y.$"] [17592186595923 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-2x}, & \\,0\\le x, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}y, & \\,1\\le y\\le 3, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X\\le 1,Y\\le 2).$"] [17592186595930 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{3}(x-1), & \\,2\\le x\\le 3, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{y}, & \\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the joint probability density function $f(x,y).$\n\n*Hint: We do not know if $X$ and $Y$ are independent.*"] [17592186595931 "The joint probability density function for the continuous random variables  $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}(1+xy),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{Y}(y)=\\frac{y+1}{3}$ for $y\\in [0,2]$\n\nII. $f(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent"] [17592186595933 "The joint probability density function for the continuous random variables  $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 3, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $$\n\\displaystyle {f}_{Y}(y)=\\frac{1}{10}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{30}\n$$ for $y\\in [0,3]$\n\nII. $f(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent"] [17592186595936 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}(x+1), & \\,0\\le x\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{y}, & \\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X\\le 1,Y\\le 1).$"] [17592186595940 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}\\sqrt{\\frac{x}{2}}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{8}{e}^{y}, & \\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡9, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(X\\le 1,0\\le Y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡9).$"] [17592186595941 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}\\,{x}^{3}, & \\,0\\le x\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}(y+2), & \\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the joint probability density function $f(x,y).$\n\n*Hint: We do not know if $X$ and $Y$ are independent.*"] [17592186595962 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 3, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{3}{2},$ find $\\text{E}[2Y-4X].$"] [17592186595965 "The joint probability density function for the continuous random variables  $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x+\\frac{3}{2}{y}^{2},\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{X}(x)=\\frac{3}{2}x+1$ for $y\\in [0,1]$\n\nII. $f(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent"] [17592186595966 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x{y}^{2}+2}{6}, & 2\\le x\\le 4,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expected value of $Y.$"] [17592186595969 "The joint probability density function for the continuous random variables  $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}xy,\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{X}(x)=2{x}^{2}+\\frac{2}{3}x$ for $x\\in [0,1]$\n\nII. $f(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent"] [17592186595971 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x, & \\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}5{y}^{4}, & \\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the joint probability density function $f(x,y).$\n\n*Hint: We do not know if $X$ and $Y$ are independent.*"] [17592186595983 "The joint probability density function for the continuous random variables  $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise.}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${f}_{Y}(y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ for $y\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]$\n\nII. $f(x,y)={f}_{X}(x)\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y)$ for all possible $x$ and $y$\n\nIII. $X$ and $Y$ are independent"] [17592186595989 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expected value of $Y.$"] [17592186595993 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}({x}^{2}+1), & \\,0\\le x\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}y, & \\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the joint probability density function $f(x,y).$"] [17592186595996 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}, & \\,1\\le x\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}4{y}^{3}, & \\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the joint probability density function $f(x,y).$"] [17592186596623 "Find the smallest natural number solution to the system of linear congruences below.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x≡0 & (\\text{mod}\\,2) \\\\ x≡4 & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ x≡7 & (\\text{mod}\\,9)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186596628 "Find the smallest natural number solution to the system of linear congruences below.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x≡1 & (\\text{mod}\\,3) \\\\ x≡3 & (\\text{mod}\\,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186596652 "Find the smallest natural number solution to the system of linear congruences below.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x≡1 & (\\text{mod}\\,2) \\\\ x≡2 & (\\text{mod}\\,3) \\\\ x≡5 & (\\text{mod}\\,7)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186596663 "According to the Chinese remainder theorem, which of the following systems of congruences has a unique solution modulo $144?$ \n\n$$\n\\text{I.}\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x≡1 & (\\text{mod}\\,3) \\\\ x≡4 & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\ x≡7 & (\\text{mod}\\,8)\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{II.}\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x≡3 & (\\text{mod}\\,9) \\\\ x≡8 & (\\text{mod}\\,16)\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{III.}\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x≡5 & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\ x≡7 & (\\text{mod}\\,24)\\end{aligned}\\,\n$$"] [17592186596670 "According to the Chinese remainder theorem, which of the following systems of congruences has a unique solution modulo $105?$ \n\n$$\n\\text{I.}\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x≡1 & (\\text{mod}\\,3) \\\\ x≡4 & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ x≡5 & (\\text{mod}\\,7)\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{II.}\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x≡3 & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ x≡6 & (\\text{mod}\\,21)\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{III.}\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x≡5 & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\ x≡7 & (\\text{mod}\\,15)\\end{aligned}\\,\n$$"] [17592186596683 "Find the smallest natural number solution to the system of linear congruences below.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x≡4 & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\ x≡8 & (\\text{mod}\\,10)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186596686 "Find the smallest natural number solution to the system of linear congruences below.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x≡1 & (\\text{mod}\\,2) \\\\ x≡4 & (\\text{mod}\\,5)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186596688 "Find the smallest natural number solution to the system of linear congruences below.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x≡1 & (\\text{mod}\\,2) \\\\ x≡3 & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ x≡5 & (\\text{mod}\\,7)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186596692 "Find the smallest natural number solution to the system of linear congruences below.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x≡2 & (\\text{mod}\\,3) \\\\ x≡4 & (\\text{mod}\\,7) \\\\ x≡5 & (\\text{mod}\\,8)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186596720 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x≡0 & (\\text{mod}\\,3) \\\\ x≡4 & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ x≡10 & (\\text{mod}\\,11)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe smallest natural-number solution to the system of linear congruences above  is $x=$\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186596722 "According to the Chinese remainder theorem, which of the following systems of congruences has a unique solution modulo $210?$ \n\n$$\n\\text{I.}\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x≡2 & (\\text{mod}\\,5) \\\\ x≡4 & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\ x≡5 & (\\text{mod}\\,7)\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{II.}\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x≡7 & (\\text{mod}\\,10) \\\\ x≡13 & (\\text{mod}\\,21)\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{III.}\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x≡9 & (\\text{mod}\\,14) \\\\ x≡11 & (\\text{mod}\\,15)\\end{aligned}\\,\n$$"] [17592186596723 "Find the smallest natural number solution to the system of linear congruences below.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x≡2 & (\\text{mod}\\,3) \\\\ x≡3 & (\\text{mod}\\,5)\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186597589 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}cxy,\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $c$ is a positive real number. Find the value of $c.$"] [17592186598565 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}xy, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,1\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $y>2.$"] [17592186598569 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}(x-y), & 2\\le x\\le 5,\\,1\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x<2,\\,y<3.$"] [17592186598571 "![](images/c6/c64cfa701997c2b9e5e54f7eb77afb9927b878065af9c32fed86f09f140a4ec4.png)\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y, & (x,y)\\in D, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y,$ find $F(-\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{1}{2}).$"] [17592186598582 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}x\\sqrt{y}, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x>2,\\,y>1.$"] [17592186598611 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}x{e}^{y}, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡4, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡4.$"] [17592186598619 "![](images/3c/3c45364ee569d9ee24bbac167ad2a76f8a0a1a519f6604b7f9427d723c59abab.png)\n\nLet $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}6y{e}^{-3x}, & (x,y)\\in D, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $F(x,y)$ is the joint CDF of $X$ and $Y,$ find $F(\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{4}{3}).$"] [17592186599232 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}(y+1),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{3}{2}$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{5}{9},$ find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$"] [17592186599256 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}y({x}^{2}+1),\\, & -1\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf $\\text{E}[X]=0$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{2}{3},$ find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$"] [17592186599790 "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<-1\\,\\text{or}\\,y<0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}({x}^{3}+1){y}^{2}, & -1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ {F}_{X}(x), & -1\\le x\\le 1,\\,y>1 \\\\ {F}_{Y}(y), & x>1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 1, & x>1,\\,y>1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${F}_{X}$ and ${F}_{Y}$ are the marginal CDFs of $X$ and $Y,$ respectively. Find the joint PDF $f(x,y)$ for $-1\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$"] [17592186599799 "The function $f(x)$ is defined below. What is the value of \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-3}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,?\n$$\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}3-x, & x<1 \\\\ 2x, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186599805 "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-1)({e}^{y}-1)}{x{e}^{y}}, & x\\ge 1,\\,y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the joint PDF $f(x,y)$ for $x\\ge 1,\\,y\\ge 0.$"] [17592186599834 "The joint CDF of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x({y}^{2}-1)}{{y}^{2}(x+3)}, & x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 1 \\\\ 0, & x<0\\,\\text{or}\\,y<1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind ${F}_{X}(x),$ the marginal CDF of $X,$ for $x\\ge 1.$"] [17592186599835 "The marginal CDFs of two continuous independent random variables $X$ and $Y$ are given below. \n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ x, & 0\\le x\\le 1 \\\\ 1, & x>1\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{F}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & y<0 \\\\ y, & 0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 1, & y>1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the joint CDF when $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$"] [17592186599842 "The marginal CDFs of two continuous independent random variables $X$ and $Y$ are given below. \n\n$$\n{F}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-{e}^{-6x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,{F}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1-{e}^{-y/3}, & y\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the joint CDF when $x\\ge 0$ and $y\\ge 0.$"] [17592186601893 "$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+y-1}{2}, & \\,0\\le 3x\\le y\\le 3 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ for the continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ is shown above. Given that \n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{7}{16},\\,\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{39}{16},\\,\\text{SD}[X]≈0.2421,\\,\\text{SD}[Y]≈0.3655,\n$$\n\n \nfind the correlation coefficient of $X$ and $Y.$ Round your answer to $2$ decimal places."] [17592186602411 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{9}x, & 0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $x>3,\\,y>2.$"] [17592186602433 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables with the joint probability density function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the expression for the corresponding joint CDF when $0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1.$"] [17592186604050 "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x-y,\\, & 1\\le x\\le 2,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{19}{12},$  $\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{5}{12},$ and $\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]=\\frac{1}{4},$ find the covariance matrix for $X$ and $Y.$"] [17592186604063 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the following conditional probability density function:\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y\\,|\\,X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x}, & \\,0<x<1,0<y<x \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $$\n\\displaystyle P(\\frac{1}{8}<Y<\\frac{1}{6}\\,|\\,X=\\frac{1}{3}).\n$$"] [17592186604573 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n=90$ from a population where $10\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the sample has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $i=1,…,90.$ If the statistic $X$ is defined as\n\n$$\nX={\\sum }_{i=1}^{90}{X}_{i}\n$$\n\nfind approximate values of $\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{Var}[X].$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.*"] [17592186604582 "There are $30$ students in the $6$th grade of Arizona Elementary School. From this population of students, $20\\%$ have blue eyes. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the population has blue eyes} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $i=1,…,30.$ Find $P({X}_{2}=0|{X}_{1}=1).$"] [17592186604604 "Suppose we have a population of size $N=230,$ where $40\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the population has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $i=1,…,230.$ Find $P({X}_{2}=0|{X}_{1}=1).$"] [17592186604607 "Suppose we have a population of size $N=120,$ where $35\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the population has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $i=1,…,120.$ Find $P({X}_{2}=0|{X}_{1}=1).$"] [17592186604617 "Suppose we have a population of size $N=150,$ where $60\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the population has the characteristic} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $i=1,…,150.$ Find $P({X}_{2}=1|{X}_{1}=0).$"] [17592186605102 "Find $\\text{E}[\\frac{X+1}{6}],$ given that the continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{18}{(x+1)^{3}}, & x\\ge 2 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186605866 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{36}{x}^{3}{y}^{2}, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 3, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $P(1\\le X\\le \\sqrt{5}).$"] [17592186605885 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{56}x(5-x),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<0 \\\\ h(x),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 4 \\\\ 1,\\, & x>4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $h(x)=${{field-1}}."] [17592186605891 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two continuous random variables with the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{4}, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe marginal CDF ${F}_{Y}(y)$ of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n{F}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & y<0 \\\\ g(y), & 0\\le y\\le 2 \\\\ 1 & y>2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the function $g(y)?$"] [17592186605910 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=3{e}^{-|6x|},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is\n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}h(x),\\, & x<0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{{e}^{-6x}}{2},\\, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $h(x)=${{field-1}}."] [17592186605925 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}},\\, & x\\ge 1 \\\\ 0,\\, & x<1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<1 \\\\ h(x),\\, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x).$"] [17592186607641 "$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,7)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0.4, & \\,y=2 \\\\ 0.6, & \\,y=3 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind $\\text{E}[Y\\,|\\,X=7]$ given that the conditional probability mass function ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,7)$ is shown above."] [17592186608203 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt[3]{x-1}, & x>2 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi (x-1)), & x\\le 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\nI. $[-3,3]$\n\nII. $[-1,1]$\n\nIII. $[1,2)$"]}
